    q: 'Who developed SynthID and when was it introduced?',
    a: 'SynthID was developed by Google DeepMind and first launched in August 2023 as a beta feature for Imagen on Vertex AI. It has since expanded to Gemini, Google Veo, Lyria music generation, and open-source Gemma text models.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can human eyes or ears detect a SynthID watermark?',
    a: 'No. SynthID is engineered to be completely imperceptible to human vision and hearing. In images and video, perturbations are restricted to sub-perceptual frequency coefficients. In audio, the watermark is concealed beneath psychoacoustic masking thresholds.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does SynthID work on text generated by Gemini and Gemma?',
    a: 'SynthID for text modifies the token generation sampling process. It uses a pseudo-random scoring function seeded by preceding token sequences to bias word selection towards designated candidate tokens, leaving a statistical trace recognizable by specialized detectors.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between SynthID and C2PA Content Credentials?',
    a: 'C2PA stores provenance data and cryptographic signing certificates inside file metadata headers, which can be easily stripped by standard photo editors or social platforms. SynthID is embedded directly within the content pixels, frequencies, or tokens, persisting through compression and format changes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can SynthID survive aggressive image compression and cropping?',
    a: 'Yes. Google DeepMind designed SynthID using a deep neural network that optimizes for robustness. The watermark survives JPEG compression down to quality level 50, spatial resizing, mild cropping, Gaussian blurring, and screenshot rasterization.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the SynthID detector publicly accessible for verification?',
    a: 'Google provides access to the SynthID detector through Vertex AI for enterprise customers, within Google Search About this Image features, and inside Gemini. Google also open-sourced the SynthID Text repository on GitHub for developer adoption.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does SynthID slow down generative model inference speeds?',
    a: 'For images, video, and audio, embedding occurs during diffusion latent sampling and adds negligible compute overhead (under 2 percent). For text generation, tournament sampling introduces minimal latency of approximately 1 to 3 milliseconds per generated token.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can SynthID watermarks be neutralized by content creators?',
    a: 'Yes. By applying targeted multi-scale frequency-domain phase dithering, tools like RemoveSynth disperse the synchronized watermark vectors below detection thresholds while preserving full visual, acoustic, and semantic fidelity.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITE.url}/` },
    { name: 'Guides', url: `${SITE.url}/tools/` },
    { name: 'What Is SynthID?', url: `${SITE.url}/what-is-synthid/` },
  ]),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
  },
  faqSchema(faqs),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema} ogType="article">
